Ammonia moves by sea mainly on fully refrigerated midsize gas carriers (MGCs), on suitable Handysize ships and, for long-haul volumes, on ammonia-capable VLGCs. Chartering it is LPG chartering with stricter checks: the ship's certification for ammonia, its previous cargoes, the time needed to switch grades and the terminal's acceptance rules. Our LPG & ammonia desk in Istanbul fixes ammonia spot, on time charter and on contracts of affreightment.

## Which ships carry ammonia?

- **[MGCs](/lpg/mgc)** (25,000–50,000 cbm) are the ammonia workhorse: fully refrigerated, and most are certified for ammonia. They trade a mix of spot voyages, 1–3 year time charters and long ammonia COAs.
- **[Handysize gas carriers](/lpg/handysize)** (15,000–25,000 cbm), semi-refrigerated or fully refrigerated, carry ammonia alongside petrochemical gases and LPG.
- **[VLGCs](/lpg/vlgc)** (70,000 cbm and above) that are ammonia-capable carry long-haul volumes.
- **[Pressurised and semi-refrigerated coasters](/lpg/pressurised)** carry small parcels where they are certified.

The IMO's International Gas Carrier Code (IGC Code) sets the requirements for ships carrying liquefied gases, and the ship's **Certificate of Fitness** lists the cargoes it may carry. Ammonia has its own requirements in the Code, including on materials it must not touch, and charterers and terminals ask about a ship's ammonia history for the same reason. We check the certificate, the last cargoes and the terminal's acceptance before a ship is offered.

## What changes when a ship switches between LPG and ammonia?

Changing grade costs time and gas. Depending on the sequence, a ship may need to warm up, gas-free, inert or aerate, then gas up and cool down before it can load, and the next terminal sets the standard it must meet. That time and those quantities are part of the freight: agree in the charter party whether the time counts, who pays for the gas used, and what happens if the terminal rejects the ship on arrival. Our note on [switching MGCs between ammonia and LPG](/blog/ammonia-on-mgcs-switching-grades-without-losing-a-voyage) works through an example.

## Spot, time charter or COA for ammonia?

- **Spot** suits irregular cargoes and is quoted in dollars per tonne.
- **Time charter** gives control of a ship for a programme, priced per month or per day; producers and traders use it when they ship regularly and want to manage the grade sequence themselves.
- **COA** gives a producer or importer predictable capacity and freight without operating a ship. The clauses that matter are the nomination periods, volume tolerances, ship approval and the freight adjustment formula.

Our guides to [voyage, time charter and COA](/guides/voyage-charter-vs-time-charter-vs-coa) and to [spot, period or COA for LPG carriers](/blog/spot-time-charter-or-coa-how-to-charter-an-lpg-carrier) compare the structures in detail. Voyages are often fixed on BIMCO's GASVOY form or on a charterer's own form; see [tanker and gas charter party forms](/guides/tanker-charter-party-forms).

## Why is ammonia drawing new charterers?

Beyond fertiliser and industrial demand, ammonia is being developed as a marine fuel and as a way to carry hydrogen, and producers of low-carbon ammonia are looking at shipping for the first time. Whatever the end use, the carriage rules are the same: certified ships, terminals that accept them, and charter terms that deal with grade changes and certification. We work with new market entrants on their first fixtures as well as with established shippers.

